  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Knowledge exchange on the use of natural predators versus chemical crop protection</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Crop protection is vital for achieving high-quality yields while minimizing the negative impacts of chemical substances on human health, animal health, and the environment. The European Green Deal and Farm-to-Fork policy emphasize the need to reduce the use and risks of crop protection products. The Netherlands Ministry of Agriculture envisions sustainable practices, including integrated crop protection and the use of BCAs and low-risk agents.Vietnam faces challenges regarding the amount and toxicity of pesticides used in horticulture. Improper use of chemicals and limited grower knowledge have led to environmental and health problems. However, there is a growing demand for safe, environmentally friendly, and organic products. The Vietnamese government aims to increase the registration and application of bioproducts and reduce pesticide residue problems.The Netherlands has been exporting BCAs to Vietnam since 2016, contributing to sustainable horticulture and farmer livelihoods. To enhance pest control options, a wider range of BCAs should be available locally.The Plant Protection Department of the Ministry for Agriculture and Rural Development in Vietnam employs an Environmental Risk Analysis model to assess the safety of introducing BCAs, but certain natural predators are considered medium risk, limiting further discussion. It is unclear if the risk analysis accounts for the risks of chemical agents currently used in Vietnam.By exploring alternative risk assessment approaches and comparing the risks of BCAs and chemical agents, the project aims to support sustainable agriculture and food system development as part of the bilateral agenda between Vietnam and the Netherlands.</narrative>
  </description>
